WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.500
Every money app plans in months.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Money runs in years.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.800
Your bank says ₹84,000.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.000
Rent, an EMI, and a March
insurance renewal say otherwise.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.200
Other apps tell you
what’s left this month.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.700
Twelve tells you
what’s safe today, and why.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.800
It plans across the next twelve months

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:22.700
and finds the bills
a monthly view never sees.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.300
One number every day, with
the whole year already taken out.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.400
Tap it, and it shows its working,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.200
including the guess
it’s least sure about.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.900
Ask it something, and it only offers
questions it can actually answer.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.500
Type an amount and watch tomorrow’s
number move before you spend.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.700
When it sees something new,
it doesn’t guess. It asks you once.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.700
After a big spend, it shows
what that did to your year,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.300
then lets you choose.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.000
Fall behind, and it shows the way back,
with the cost of each path.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.000
And every month, it marks its own
homework and gets sharper.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.800
Every screen answers one question:

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.300
Am I okay?

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.800
Deep forest for the app.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.350
Warm cream whenever Twelve speaks.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:22.100
Twelve, a concept by Vishal Yadav.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.200
The full case study is below.
